.TH BALROGG 1 "2026-09-03" "balrogg 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
balrogg \- lossless Ogg Vorbis and Ogg Opus recompressor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B balrogg
.RB [ \-1 .. \-9 ]
.RB [ \-p ]
.B e
.I input output
.br
.B balrogg
.RB [ \-p ]
.B d
.I input output
.br
.B balrogg
.RB [ \-1 .. \-9 ]
.RB [ \-p ]
.RB [ \-j
.IR N ]
.B \-b
.BR e | d
.IR file ...
.br
.B balrogg
.B dump
.I archive
.br
.B balrogg
.B pages
.I oggfile
.br
.B balrogg
.BR \-h | \-v
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B balrogg
losslessly recompresses Ogg Vorbis and Opus files. Expanding an archive restores
the original bytes.
.PP
Both verbs detect the codec from the input.
.B e
uses the first Ogg page and
.B d
uses the archive header.
.SH VERBS
.TP
.B e
Compress
.I input
to
.IR output .
Files that cannot be reproduced exactly are refused.
.TP
.B d
Expand the archive
.I input
to
.IR output .
.TP
.B dump
Print an archive's header, tune and stream lengths.
.TP
.B pages
Print every page of an Ogg file and whether it can be reframed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-1 " .. " \-9
Effort.
.B \-9
is the default. Through
.B \-4
each level adds a residue-model stage and affects decoding. Above
.B \-4
only the encoder's parameter search grows. The archive stores those parameters,
so decoding runs at one speed from
.B \-4
.IR onward .
Tuning evaluates the whole file. The best encoded candidate is retained
without an additional encode pass to write the winner.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-progress
Show encoding, decoding and individual Vorbis tuning progress bars on stderr.
Progress is off by default. The final phase reaches 100 percent only after
the output is written. Batch mode uses separate lines labeled by input file.
.TP
.B \-\-progress-lines
Write progress checkpoints as separate lines, suitable for log files.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-batch
Every remaining argument is an input. Encoding writes
.IR file .blr
beside each input and decoding strips the suffix, or appends
.B .out
when there is none. Files run in separate processes within CPU and memory
limits. Larger inputs start first. Remaining files continue after a refusal.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs = \fIN\fR
Run
.I N
files at once instead of measuring the machine.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version, build host, and source libopus release.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Malformed, unsupported, or unrecognized input. In batch mode, at least one
file was refused and no higher status occurred.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
A file could not be opened, read or written.
.TP
.B 4
Internal error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BLR_MEMCAP
Per-process memory cap in MiB. The default is 2048. A value of 0 disables it.
Sanitizer builds omit the cap.
.TP
.B BLR_SIMD
Select the context-mixer kernel on x86.
.B scalar
forces the portable kernel and
.B sse2
refuses to run unless the SSE2 kernel is built and the CPU supports it.
SSE2 is the default when CPUID reports it.
.SH LIMITS
Input and output must be seekable.
.PP
Opus mode supports one mono or stereo logical stream with channel mapping
family 0, with packets (including headers) up to 61,440 bytes.
Vorbis floor type 0 and codebook lookup type 2 are unsupported.
Pages with bad checksums and files without a final end-of-stream page are also
refused.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
balrogg e track.ogg track.blr
balrogg d track.blr track.ogg
balrogg -5 -b e album/*.ogg
balrogg -b d album/*.blr
.fi
